/-!
# Equation-scaling concentration for the nonlinear heat equation

The nonlinear-heat residual of the uncorrected free-scale ansatz

`u(x,t) = λ(t)^(-2/(p-1)) Q(λ(t)⁻¹ • (x-x₀))`.

The residual is the sum of two terms:

* the stationary elliptic residual `ΔQ + Qᵖ`;
* the modulation defect generated by the time-dependent scale `λ`.

In particular, solving the elliptic profile equation does not make a moving-scale ansatz solve
the parabolic equation. For a stationary profile positive at the origin, an ansatz that solves
the equation at its center throughout a connected open time set has constant scale
(`HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.scale_eq_of_equation_residual_eq_zero_at_center`). For a
nonnegative stationary profile, the residual at one time vanishes everywhere exactly when the
scale is instantaneously stationary or the scaling generator annihilates the profile
(`HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.forall_equation_residual_eq_zero_iff`).

The field itself concentrates: for a nonzero profile and a positive scale tending to zero, the
extended spatial supremum diverges, and a bounded profile gives finite-time blow-up
(`scaling_invariant_concentration_ansatz_blows_up_at`).

The exponent `2 / (p - 1)` is the one fixed by the scaling of the nonlinear heat equation. Type II
blow-up would further require a solution with this leading profile whose scale is asymptotically
smaller than the Type I scale `sqrt (T - t)`.

Regularity, nonnegativity, `p ≠ 1`, and the conditions on the scale are hypotheses of the
individual theorems; the norm-divergence theorem also assumes `p > 1`.

The modulation viewpoint occurs, for example, in the type II constructions of Schweyer and of
Collot, whose solutions concentrate a stationary profile at a scale `λ(t)`; the leading term of
Collot's solutions is `λ(t)^(-2/(p-1)) Q((x - x₀)/λ(t))`. The identities here are the
substitution algebra of the uncorrected pure-profile ansatz, without a correction term.

## References

* R. Schweyer, "Type II blow-up for the four dimensional energy critical semi linear heat
  equation", *J. Funct. Anal.* 263 (2012), 3922-3983, arXiv:1201.2907.
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfa.2012.09.015>
* C. Collot, "Nonradial type II blow up for the energy-supercritical semilinear heat
  equation", *Anal. PDE* 10 (2017), 127-252, arXiv:1604.02856.
  <https://doi.org/10.2140/apde.2017.10.127>
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace PDE.Solution.Singularity.FiniteTime.NonlinearHeat

open Filter Topology
open PDE.Representations.Similarity

variable {n : ℕ} (p : ℝ)

/-- The equation-scaling invariant concentration ansatz
`λ(t)^(-2/(p-1)) Q (λ(t)⁻¹ • (x-x₀))`.

The singular time is not stored because it does not occur in the formula. Theorems using the
ansatz supply their own time domain and hypotheses on the positive scale `λ`. -/
noncomputable def scalingInvariantConcentrationAnsatz
    (profile : ℝ^n → ℝ) (scale : ℝ → ℝ) (x₀ : ℝ^n)
    (x : ℝ^n) (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  concentrationAnsatz profile scale (2 / (p - 1)) x₀ x t

/-- The stationary elliptic residual associated with equation-scaling concentration. -/
noncomputable def stationaryProfileResidual
    (profile : ℝ^n → ℝ) (y : ℝ^n) : ℝ :=
  laplacian profile y + profile y ^ p

/-- The stationary concentration residual vanishes pointwise.

This predicate does not assert that `profile` is differentiable. Theorems interpreting it as a
classical elliptic equation supply `C²` regularity separately. -/
def HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual (profile : ℝ^n → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ y, stationaryProfileResidual p profile y = 0

/-- A profile with zero stationary residual satisfies `ΔQ + Qᵖ = 0` pointwise. -/
theorem HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.equation
    {profile : ℝ^n → ℝ}
    (hequation : HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual (n := n) p profile)
    (y : ℝ^n) :
    laplacian profile y + profile y ^ p = 0 := by
  simpa only [stationaryProfileResidual] using hequation y

/-- The equation-scaling parabolic residual is the stationary profile residual plus the
modulation defect.

The balance identity
`(2 / (p - 1)) * p = 2 / (p - 1) + 2`
aligns diffusion and reaction. Nonnegativity is needed only at the displayed similarity
coordinate to split the real power of the amplitude-profile product. -/
theorem equation_residual_scaling_invariant_concentration_ansatz
    (profile : ℝ^n → ℝ) (scale : ℝ → ℝ)
    (x₀ x : ℝ^n) (t : ℝ)
    (hprofile : ContDiff ℝ 2 profile)
    (hnonneg :
      0 ≤ profile (centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x))
    (hscale : DifferentiableAt ℝ scale t)
    (hscale_pos : 0 < scale t)
    (hp : p ≠ 1) :
    equationResidual p (scalingInvariantConcentrationAnsatz p profile scale x₀) x t =
      scale t ^ (-(2 / (p - 1) + 2)) *
        (stationaryProfileResidual p profile
            (centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x) +
          scale t * deriv scale t *
            scalingGenerator (2 / (p - 1)) 1 profile
              (centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x)) := by
  have hbalance :
      (2 / (p - 1)) * p = 2 / (p - 1) + 2 := by
    field_simp [sub_ne_zero.mpr hp]
    ring
  change
    equationResidual p
        (concentrationAnsatz profile scale (2 / (p - 1)) x₀) x t =
      _
  simpa only [stationaryProfileResidual] using
    equation_residual_concentration_ansatz
      (n := n) p profile scale (2 / (p - 1)) x₀ x t
      hprofile hnonneg hscale hscale_pos hbalance

/-- For a stationary profile, the only remaining parabolic error is the modulation
defect generated by `λ'(t)`. -/
theorem HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.equation_residual_eq_modulation_defect
    {profile : ℝ^n → ℝ}
    (hequation : HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual (n := n) p profile)
    (scale : ℝ → ℝ) (x₀ x : ℝ^n) (t : ℝ)
    (hprofile : ContDiff ℝ 2 profile)
    (hnonneg :
      0 ≤ profile (centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x))
    (hscale : DifferentiableAt ℝ scale t)
    (hscale_pos : 0 < scale t)
    (hp : p ≠ 1) :
    equationResidual p (scalingInvariantConcentrationAnsatz p profile scale x₀) x t =
      scale t ^ (-(2 / (p - 1) + 2)) *
        (scale t * deriv scale t *
          scalingGenerator (2 / (p - 1)) 1 profile
            (centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x)) := by
  rw [equation_residual_scaling_invariant_concentration_ansatz
    p profile scale x₀ x t
    hprofile hnonneg hscale hscale_pos hp]
  rw [hequation]
  ring

/-- Exact criterion for the pure concentration ansatz to solve the equation at one point.

For a stationary elliptic profile and a positive scale, the residual can vanish only because
the scale is instantaneously stationary or because the profile is annihilated by the scaling
generator at the corresponding similarity coordinate. -/
theorem HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.equation_residual_eq_zero_iff
    {profile : ℝ^n → ℝ}
    (hequation : HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual (n := n) p profile)
    (scale : ℝ → ℝ) (x₀ x : ℝ^n) (t : ℝ)
    (hprofile : ContDiff ℝ 2 profile)
    (hnonneg :
      0 ≤ profile (centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x))
    (hscale : DifferentiableAt ℝ scale t)
    (hscale_pos : 0 < scale t)
    (hp : p ≠ 1) :
    equationResidual p
        (scalingInvariantConcentrationAnsatz p profile scale x₀) x t =
      0 ↔
      deriv scale t = 0 ∨
        scalingGenerator (2 / (p - 1)) 1 profile
          (centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x) = 0 := by
  rw [HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.equation_residual_eq_modulation_defect
    (p := p) hequation scale x₀ x t hprofile hnonneg hscale hscale_pos hp]
  have hfactor_ne :
      scale t ^ (-(2 / (p - 1) + 2)) ≠ 0 :=
    (Real.rpow_pos_of_pos hscale_pos _).ne'
  have hscale_ne : scale t ≠ 0 :=
    ne_of_gt hscale_pos
  constructor
  · intro hzero
    have hmodulation :
        scale t * deriv scale t *
            scalingGenerator (2 / (p - 1)) 1 profile
              (centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x) =
          0 :=
      (mul_eq_zero.mp hzero).resolve_left hfactor_ne
    rcases mul_eq_zero.mp hmodulation with hscale_deriv | hgenerator
    · exact Or.inl ((mul_eq_zero.mp hscale_deriv).resolve_left hscale_ne)
    · exact Or.inr hgenerator
  · rintro (hscale_deriv | hgenerator)
    · simp [hscale_deriv]
    · simp [hgenerator]

/-! ## Rigidity of a pure moving scale -/

/-- If a stationary profile is positive at the origin and its pure concentration ansatz solves
the equation at the center, then the scale is instantaneously stationary.

At `x = x₀`, the similarity coordinate is zero and the Euler part of `scalingGenerator`
vanishes. The alternative in `equation_residual_eq_zero_iff` would therefore force
`(2 / (p - 1)) * profile 0 = 0`, contradicting positivity. -/
theorem HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.deriv_scale_eq_zero_of_equation_residual_eq_zero_at_center
    {profile : ℝ^n → ℝ}
    (hequation : HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual (n := n) p profile)
    (scale : ℝ → ℝ) (x₀ : ℝ^n) (t : ℝ)
    (hprofile : ContDiff ℝ 2 profile)
    (hprofile_pos : 0 < profile 0)
    (hscale : DifferentiableAt ℝ scale t)
    (hscale_pos : 0 < scale t)
    (hp : p ≠ 1)
    (hresidual :
      equationResidual p
          (scalingInvariantConcentrationAnsatz p profile scale x₀) x₀ t =
        0) :
    deriv scale t = 0 := by
  have hcoordinate :
      centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x₀ = 0 := by
    simp [centeredDilation]
  rcases
      (HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.equation_residual_eq_zero_iff
        (p := p) hequation scale x₀ x₀ t hprofile
        (hcoordinate ▸ hprofile_pos.le) hscale hscale_pos hp).1 hresidual with
    hderiv | hgenerator
  · exact hderiv
  · have hcoefficient : 2 / (p - 1) ≠ 0 :=
      div_ne_zero (by norm_num) (sub_ne_zero.mpr hp)
    have hprofile_zero : profile 0 = 0 := by
      apply (mul_eq_zero.mp ?_).resolve_left hcoefficient
      simpa [scalingGenerator, hcoordinate] using hgenerator
    exact (hprofile_pos.ne' hprofile_zero).elim

/-- A pure concentration ansatz that solves the equation at its center throughout a connected
open time set has constant scale.

The pointwise center-rigidity theorem makes the scale derivative vanish at every time. The
mean-value theorem then upgrades this infinitesimal statement to equality of any two scale
values in the time set. -/
theorem HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.scale_eq_of_equation_residual_eq_zero_at_center
    {profile : ℝ^n → ℝ}
    (hequation : HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual (n := n) p profile)
    (scale : ℝ → ℝ) (x₀ : ℝ^n) (timeSet : Set ℝ)
    (hprofile : ContDiff ℝ 2 profile)
    (hprofile_pos : 0 < profile 0)
    (htime_open : IsOpen timeSet)
    (htime_preconnected : IsPreconnected timeSet)
    (hscale : DifferentiableOn ℝ scale timeSet)
    (hscale_pos : ∀ t ∈ timeSet, 0 < scale t)
    (hp : p ≠ 1)
    (hresidual :
      ∀ t ∈ timeSet,
        equationResidual p
            (scalingInvariantConcentrationAnsatz p profile scale x₀) x₀ t =
          0)
    {s t : ℝ} (hs : s ∈ timeSet) (ht : t ∈ timeSet) :
    scale s = scale t := by
  apply htime_open.is_const_of_deriv_eq_zero htime_preconnected hscale ?_ hs ht
  intro τ hτ
  exact
    HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.deriv_scale_eq_zero_of_equation_residual_eq_zero_at_center
      (p := p) hequation scale x₀ τ hprofile hprofile_pos
      (hscale.differentiableAt (htime_open.mem_nhds hτ))
      (hscale_pos τ hτ) hp (hresidual τ hτ)

/-- At a fixed time, the pure concentration ansatz has zero residual at every spatial point
exactly when either the scale is instantaneously stationary or the stationary profile is
annihilated everywhere by the nonlinear-heat scaling generator.

The reverse direction uses the pointwise criterion. For the forward direction, positivity of the
scale makes the similarity-coordinate map surjective, so inverse dilation reaches every profile
coordinate. -/
theorem HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.forall_equation_residual_eq_zero_iff
    {profile : ℝ^n → ℝ}
    (hequation : HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual (n := n) p profile)
    (scale : ℝ → ℝ) (x₀ : ℝ^n) (t : ℝ)
    (hprofile : ContDiff ℝ 2 profile)
    (hnonneg : ∀ y, 0 ≤ profile y)
    (hscale : DifferentiableAt ℝ scale t)
    (hscale_pos : 0 < scale t)
    (hp : p ≠ 1) :
    (∀ x,
      equationResidual p
          (scalingInvariantConcentrationAnsatz p profile scale x₀) x t =
        0) ↔
      deriv scale t = 0 ∨
        ∀ y, scalingGenerator (2 / (p - 1)) 1 profile y = 0 := by
  constructor
  · intro hresidual
    by_cases hderiv : deriv scale t = 0
    · exact Or.inl hderiv
    · right
      intro y
      have hspatial_ne : (scale t)⁻¹ ≠ 0 :=
        inv_ne_zero (ne_of_gt hscale_pos)
      let x := x₀ + ((scale t)⁻¹)⁻¹ • y
      have hcoordinate :
          centeredDilation (scale t)⁻¹ x₀ x = y :=
        centered_dilation_center_add_inv_smul hspatial_ne x₀ y
      have hpoint :=
        (HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.equation_residual_eq_zero_iff
          (p := p) hequation scale x₀ x t hprofile
          (hnonneg _) hscale hscale_pos hp).1 (hresidual x)
      simpa only [hcoordinate] using hpoint.resolve_left hderiv
  · rintro (hderiv | hgenerator) x
    · exact
        (HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.equation_residual_eq_zero_iff
          (p := p) hequation scale x₀ x t hprofile
          (hnonneg _) hscale hscale_pos hp).2 (Or.inl hderiv)
    · exact
        (HasZeroStationaryProfileResidual.equation_residual_eq_zero_iff
          (p := p) hequation scale x₀ x t hprofile
          (hnonneg _) hscale hscale_pos hp).2 (Or.inr (hgenerator _))

/-- A nonzero equation-scaling profile has divergent extended spatial supremum whenever its
positive scale tends to zero. No boundedness of the profile is required for this limit.

This statement concerns the field defined by the ansatz. The residual theorems above explain
why a moving pure profile generally needs correction terms before it becomes an exact solution. -/
theorem tendsto_spatial_sup_norm_scaling_invariant_concentration_ansatz_nhds_top
    (profile : ℝ^n → ℝ) (scale : ℝ → ℝ) (T : ℝ) (x₀ : ℝ^n)
    (hp : 1 < p)
    (y : ℝ^n) (hy : profile y ≠ 0)
    (hscale :
      Tendsto scale (nhdsWithin T (Set.Iio T)) (nhdsWithin 0 (Set.Ioi 0))) :
    Tendsto (spatialSupNorm (scalingInvariantConcentrationAnsatz p profile scale x₀))
      (nhdsWithin T (Set.Iio T)) (nhds ⊤) := by
  have hscale_pos :
      ∀ᶠ t in nhdsWithin T (Set.Iio T), 0 < scale t :=
    hscale.eventually self_mem_nhdsWithin
  apply tendsto_spatial_sup_norm_scaled_profile_ansatz_nhds_top
    profile
    (fun s ↦ scale s ^ (-(2 / (p - 1))))
    (fun s ↦ (scale s)⁻¹) x₀ y
  · filter_upwards [hscale_pos] with t ht
    exact inv_ne_zero (ne_of_gt ht)
  · have hexponent : -(2 / (p - 1)) < 0 :=
      neg_lt_zero.mpr (div_pos (by norm_num) (sub_pos.mpr hp))
    have hamplitude :
        Tendsto (fun t ↦ scale t ^ (-(2 / (p - 1))))
          (nhdsWithin T (Set.Iio T)) atTop :=
      (tendsto_rpow_neg_nhdsGT_zero hexponent).comp hscale
    have hproduct :
        Tendsto
          (fun t ↦ scale t ^ (-(2 / (p - 1))) * ‖profile y‖)
          (nhdsWithin T (Set.Iio T)) atTop :=
      Filter.Tendsto.atTop_mul_const (norm_pos_iff.mpr hy) hamplitude
    refine Filter.Tendsto.congr' ?_ hproduct
    filter_upwards [hscale_pos] with t ht
    simp only [norm_smul, Real.norm_eq_abs]
    rw [abs_of_pos (Real.rpow_pos_of_pos ht _)]

/-- A bounded nonzero equation-scaling profile blows up when its positive scale tends to zero.
The profile bound makes each spatial slice finite; the residual criteria remain separate. -/
theorem scaling_invariant_concentration_ansatz_blows_up_at
    (profile : ℝ^n → ℝ) (scale : ℝ → ℝ) (T : ℝ) (x₀ : ℝ^n)
    (hp : 1 < p)
    (y : ℝ^n) (hy : profile y ≠ 0)
    (hscale :
      Tendsto scale (nhdsWithin T (Set.Iio T)) (nhdsWithin 0 (Set.Ioi 0)))
    (hbounded : Bornology.IsBounded (Set.range profile)) :
    SpatialSupNormBlowsUpAt
      (scalingInvariantConcentrationAnsatz p profile scale x₀) T :=
  ⟨tendsto_spatial_sup_norm_scaling_invariant_concentration_ansatz_nhds_top
      p profile scale T x₀ hp y hy hscale,
    Filter.Eventually.of_forall fun t ↦
      spatial_sup_norm_scaled_profile_ansatz_ne_top profile
        (fun s ↦ scale s ^ (-(2 / (p - 1)))) (fun s ↦ (scale s)⁻¹) x₀ t hbounded⟩

end PDE.Solution.Singularity.FiniteTime.NonlinearHeat
